        "exercise-snacks-explained",
        "train",
        "Exercise snacks: the two-minute habit that counts",
        "Short, hard-ish bursts scattered through the day - stairs, sprints, carried loads - and the evidence that bouts of any length count toward your activity.",
        "<p>The most useful idea in modern activity science is embarrassingly simple: <b>exercise does not need to arrive in 45-minute blocks</b>. Short bursts of movement scattered through an ordinary day &mdash; what researchers call exercise snacks &mdash; count, accumulate, and measurably improve fitness and health. For anyone who &ldquo;cannot find the time&rdquo;, this is the unlock.</p>"
        "<h2>What counts as an exercise snack</h2>"
        "<p>An exercise snack is a burst of one to a few minutes at a pace that genuinely raises your breathing: three flights of stairs briskly, a 60-second all-out effort on a bike or hill, twenty bodyweight squats between meetings, a fast walk to the far shop instead of the near one, carrying the shopping in one heavy trip. The defining feature is <b>intensity, not duration</b> &mdash; a two-minute bout you could not hold a conversation through does more per minute than twenty minutes of strolling.</p>"
        "<h2>Why the evidence backs it</h2>"
        "<p>Two findings matter. First, the big guideline change: current physical-activity guidance &mdash; the WHO's activity recommendations and the science behind them &mdash; dropped the old rule that bouts had to last at least ten minutes to count. <b>Every minute counts toward the 150</b>; accumulation is accumulation. Second, the snack-specific studies: stair-climbing protocols of three hard climbs a day, three days a week, improved cardiorespiratory fitness in sedentary young adults within six weeks, and similar short-burst protocols have shown real VO2max and strength gains. The effects are modest &mdash; snacks are not a substitute for structured training &mdash; but for a person currently doing nothing, they are the difference between zero and a genuine dose.</p>"
        "<h2>Snacks versus sessions: the honest split</h2>"
        "<ul>"
        "<li><b>Snacks win on adherence</b> &mdash; they attach to existing moments (the kettle boils, the call ends, the bus is late) and need no kit, travel or changing.</li>"
        "<li><b>Sessions win on ceiling</b> &mdash; progressive strength and serious aerobic development need the structure the plans on this desk provide. Snacks maintain and nudge; programmes build.</li>"
        "<li><b>Together they compound</b> &mdash; a walker who adds stair snacks, or a lifter who stays loose between sessions, moves more total minutes without ever &ldquo;finding time&rdquo;.</li>"
        "</ul>"
        "<h2>A starter plate</h2>"
        "<p>Pick three moments you already have: <b>morning</b> &mdash; two minutes of brisk stairs before the shower; <b>midday</b> &mdash; a fast six-minute walk after lunch (digestion and daylight included); <b>evening</b> &mdash; a two-minute circuit (squats, push-ups against a wall, step-ups) while something boils or buffers. Three snacks a day is roughly the movement of a short workout, earned in six scattered minutes. Scale up by adding snacks, not length &mdash; and when the habit holds, the <a href=\"/fitness/how-to-start-working-out/\">starting guide</a> and the <a href=\"/fitness/30-day-walking-plan/\">walking plan</a> are the natural next structure. The evidence is unusually kind to small things: every minute counts, and two minutes is never nothing.</p>",
