        "how-to-choose-running-shoes",
        "choose",
        "How to choose running shoes without the marketing",
        "Comfort first, fit second, gait-analysis myths third: a practical, evidence-honest guide to picking running shoes &mdash; and when expensive actually matters.",
        "<p>Running-shoe marketing has two favourite villains: your foot and your wallet. Pronation is framed as a defect, cushioning as a technology race, and the perfect shoe as something a £200 gait analysis will reveal. The research tells a quieter story, and the practical guidance that follows from it is refreshingly cheap.</p>"
        "<h2>The one rule the evidence supports</h2>"
        "<p>Multiple studies on shoe prescription have converged on an uncomfortable finding for the industry: matching shoes to foot type or arch height <b>does not reliably reduce injury rates</b>. The filter that keeps surviving in the research is <b>comfort</b> &mdash; when runners pick the shoe that feels best, injury rates trend lower than when shoes are assigned by measurements. Practical translation: the right shoe is the one you forget you are wearing after ten minutes, not the one the chart says you deserve.</p>"
        "<h2>Fit before features</h2>"
        "<ul>"
        "<li><b>Length:</b> a thumb's width (roughly 1&ndash;1.5 cm) between longest toe and the end &mdash; feet swell and slide over distance. Try shoes in the afternoon, when feet are largest.</li>"
        "<li><b>Width:</b> no pinching at the little toe, no heel slip when walking briskly. Sizing varies between brands by up to a full size; ignore your &ldquo;usual&rdquo; number.</li>"
        "<li><b>Purpose:</b> daily trainers for easy miles, a lighter pair for fast days if you race &mdash; but one good daily trainer covers almost every beginner completely.</li>"
        "<li><b>Surface:</b> road shoes for pavement, trail shoes only for actual trails (the grippy rubber wears fast and feels vague on tarmac).</li>"
        "</ul>"
        "<h2>What the categories actually mean</h2>"
        "<p><b>Neutral</b> shoes let the foot move; <b>stability</b> shoes add medial support for pronounced overpronation. Overpronation itself is normal &mdash; most runners do it, and it only becomes interesting alongside pain or very large ranges, which is a physio conversation, not a shoe purchase. <b>Max-cushion</b> shoes are comfortable and fine for easy miles; <b>carbon-plated racers</b> genuinely improve running economy in racing, and are a waste on training runs and on every beginner's wallet. Drop (heel-to-toe height difference) matters less than marketing suggests &mdash; change it gradually if at all, because tissues adapt slowly.</p>"
        "<h2>When to replace, what to spend</h2>"
        "<p>The honest replacement range is roughly <b>500&ndash;800 km</b>, or when the midsole feels dead and legs feel beaten after easy runs &mdash; tread wear is a poor guide. Price-wise, last season's model of a good shoe is routinely 30&ndash;50% off and is the same shoe; the mid-range is where the value lives, and the flagship premium is mostly buying looks. Whatever you choose, ramp mileage sensibly &mdash; the strongest injury predictor in the research is training-load spikes, not footwear. Walk into the plan with the <a href=\"/fitness/beginner-running-plan/\">beginner running plan</a>, and let the shoes be the easy part.</p>",
