    (
        "couch-to-5k-explained",
        "train",
        "Couch to 5K: why the walk-run method works",
        "The run-walk progression that took millions from sofa to 5K: why the method works, how to adapt the classic nine weeks, and the mistakes that end week three.",
        "<p>Couch to 5K &mdash; a structured walk-run progression from zero running to 5 kilometres &mdash; is the most successful beginner running programme ever made, and its genius has nothing to do with running. It is a pacing and adaptation design disguised as a running plan, and understanding why it works makes you far more likely to finish it.</p>"
        "<h2>The core mechanism: intervals before you know it</h2>"
        "<p>The classic plan alternates running bursts with walking recovery &mdash; week one might be 60 seconds of running, 90 seconds of walking, repeated &mdash; and each week shifts the ratio until week nine is a continuous 30-minute jog. This is <b>interval training for people who do not know they are doing intervals</b>: the walk breaks hold heart rate and impact below the level that breaks beginners, while total running volume climbs steadily. Your bones, tendons and joints adapt far more slowly than your lungs &mdash; the walk breaks are the plan respecting that timeline. The CDC's physical-activity guidance for adults (150 minutes of moderate activity weekly, built up gradually) is exactly the dose the finished plan delivers.</p>"
        "<h2>The three rules that make or break it</h2>"
        "<ul>"
        "<li><b>Run the runs slow.</b> Conversational pace &mdash; if you cannot talk in sentences, you are racing the plan. The single most common failure is running the easy runs too fast, turning recovery gaps into debt.</li>"
        "<li><b>Never repeat a week out of pride, never skip one out of impatience.</b> The schedule assumes adaptation rates that hold for almost everyone; both deviations break the ramp.</li>"
        "<li><b>Three sessions a week, rest days between.</b> The adaptation happens on the rest days &mdash; the recovery science is in the <a href=\"/fitness/rest-days-why-muscles-grow/\">rest-days guide</a>.</li>"
        "</ul>"
        "<h2>Adapting the classic nine weeks</h2>"
        "<p>The published plan is a template, not scripture. Carrying extra weight, returning from injury or starting from very low fitness? Repeat each phase once &mdash; eighteen weeks to a 5K is not slow, it is finished. Knees unhappy? Swap one weekly run for cycling or the rower and keep the pattern. The goal of the plan is a <b>continuous, comfortable 30-minute run</b>; the distance label is a by-product. Most people who complete it discover their &ldquo;5K&rdquo; is closer to 4&ndash;5 km depending on pace &mdash; irrelevant, and liberating.</p>"
        "<h2>What week three actually feels like</h2>"
        "<p>Week three is the classic dropout point: novelty gone, fitness not yet visible, shins mildly annoyed. The honest briefing: this is the adaptation trough, it passes by week five for nearly everyone who keeps the pace easy, and the first day the run segments feel <i>easy</i> arrives sooner than you expect. Finish the nine weeks and you join the largest club in running &mdash; people who once could not run a minute. Then the next question (&ldquo;10K?&rdquo;) takes care of itself, one slow week at a time.</p>",
    ),
